 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 529839,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Estimate the standard deviations of this normal distribution, centered at 5: <br>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"exercises/media/ed77ce53a8928eb1.png\" alt=\"A normal distribution curve. The horizontal axis ranges from negative 10 to 20, in increments of 2. The curve begins before negative 10, has a peak value at 5, and ends after 20.\">\n</figure>",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>Step 1: Draw a line down from the peak.</p>\n<p>You are told the peak is at 5.</p>\n<p>Step 2: Starting from the peak, notice where the curve changes from curving down to curving up. Mark the point where that curvature changes, the inflection point. Draw a line down from that point. It could help you to hold an index card or other object with a perpendicular edge up to the screen.</p>\n<p>It looks like the inflection point is around 11.</p>\n<p>Step 3: The distance between the two numbers along the horizontal axis is an estimate of the standard deviation.</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"11 - 5 = 6\"></span></p>\n<p>The estimate of the standard deviation is 6.</p>\n<p>You might have been off a bit because of the difficulty of judging where the inflection point is.</p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "6"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
